body:
## What

Chinese commercial documents print the total in capital numerals (大写金额) beside the figures. Quotes, contracts, order confirmations and invoices all do this, and a buyer's finance team expects it. For example, `245000.00` is printed as `贰拾肆万伍仟元整`, and `10500.5` as `壹万零伍佰元伍角`.

The formula stdlib on main has no function for this. The full list is: abs, ceil, coalesce, contains, date, datetime, daysBetween, endsWith, floor, isBlank, isEmpty, isoDate, isoDatetime, joinNonEmpty, label, len, lower, matches, max, min, now, round, startsWith, trim, upper. None of them turns a number into Chinese text.

## Why not app-side

Hand-writing it in CEL is long and easy to get wrong:
- the zero rules (零 between groups, no doubled 零, none at the end);
- the 万 and 亿 groups;
- 角 and 分 with 整;
- negatives and rounding.

Every app that prints a Chinese document would have to copy that logic. It belongs in the stdlib once, with tests.

## Ask

Add a formula function, for example `rmbUpper(number): string` (any name is fine). Requirements:
- it follows the usual 人民币大写 rules, including 零 handling across 万 and 亿, 元 / 角 / 分 and 整;
- it rounds to 分;
- it handles 0 (`零元整`) and negative values (`负…`);
- it can be used in formula fields, so a print page or `record:details` can show it.

## Consumer

An app project's printed quote (`quote_print_page`, `record:details` totals) needs a 「大写金额」 line under the total. The app will not hand-roll this.
